<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Yenisei Kyrgyz Khaganate</h1><p class="st-meta">840 CE – 924 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Turkic people of the upper Yenisei who destroyed the Uyghur Khaganate in 840 and briefly ruled the Mongolian steppe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yenisei-kyrgyz">Kyrgyz</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minusinsk-basin">Minusinsk basin</a> on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yenisei-river">Yenisei</a> were known to Chinese sources for centuries, subject in turn to the Turkic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagans</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/uyghur-khaganate">Uyghur Khaganate</a>. After a long war, in 840 a Kyrgyz army allied with a dissident <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> general sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordu-baliq">Ordu-Baliq</a>, scattering the Uyghurs.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yenisei-kyrgyz">Kyrgyz</a> ruler, titled ajo or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a>, ruled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yenisei-river">Yenisei</a> and the Mongolian steppe, sending embassies to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> court. The Kyrgyz combined herding, farming and iron working, and left many stelae in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-turkic">Old Turkic</a> runic script along the Yenisei. Chinese sources describe them as tall, with red hair and green eyes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yenisei-kyrgyz">Kyrgyz</a> never fully moved their centre to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. In 924 the Khitan <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a> drove them out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkhon-river">Orkhon</a> region, and they withdrew to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yenisei-river">Yenisei</a>. Their descendants are linked to the modern Kyrgyz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khakas-people">Khakas</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/yenisei-kyrgyz-khaganate.jpg" alt="Yenisei Kyrgyz Khaganate" width="320" height="358"><figcaption>Image: Andrey Astaykin · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Great_Kyrgyz_Khaganate_9th_century._Atlas_Tartarica.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>840 CE – 924 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tengrism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Kyrgyz (Turkic)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>, <a href="/countries/mongolia">Mongolia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kyrgyz Khaganate, Yenisei Kirghiz</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=882&amp;focus=yenisei-kyrgyz-khaganate" aria-label="Yenisei Kyrgyz Khaganate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7080 514 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Yenisei Kyrgyz Khaganate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7400 770L7400 770"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7400 770h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7400 770h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7400 770h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=882&amp;focus=yenisei-kyrgyz-khaganate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
